    "## 10. How to read this, and what to do next\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Does anything beat \"Last 28 days\"?** Check WAPE *and* the share of origins where it wins. If the per-group choice (leave-one-out) beats it on most of the 26 origins, the model is adding something.\n",
    "2. **Did the second year help?** Compare the two *last-year change* rows and the LightGBM rows with Last 28 days, overall and by quarter. Seasonality should matter most around the holidays.\n",
    "3. **Which groups gain?** Steady SKUs should be close to last month; gains, if any, come from Variable and Erratic. Rare SKUs are mostly luck at a monthly horizon.\n",
    "4. **Use the range, not just the number.** For Erratic and Rare SKUs the 80% range is wide, and that is the real message.\n",
    "5. **Two pick counts.** `expected_picks_28d` counts every pick. The slotting algorithm only counts pick-face case picks at mapped locations, so for that use `expected_case_picks_28d` (or ask for the case-pick count to be forecast directly with the same method).\n",
    "6. **Next ideas:** order-level data (open orders, order-received dates) as leading features; the SKUs picked together on the same order (what the slotting detour depends on); try `CALENDAR_FEATURES = True` and compare.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Caveats:** these are pick counts, not customer demand; stockouts can depress historical picks; zero-fill after a SKU's last pick is an assumption; the LightGBM settings are reasonable defaults and are not tuned; two years gives each calendar month only twice, so month-specific effects are still weakly learned; the group thresholds (0.4 / 0.8 / 5%) are design choices."
